macOS 26.2 <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/11/12/apple-macos-tahoe-26-2-edge-light/">added an Edge Light feature to Macs</a> with the M1 chip or newer, and it has now been rumored that this feature will expand to the iPhone.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/11/apple-edge-light-macos-tahoe-26-2.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1293" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1026659" /><br/>
According to <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2098134121071411243">pdfu</a>, who has been digging through code in Apple's software updates in recent weeks, the iOS 27 Release Candidate contains evidence of the Edge Light feature coming to the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/apple-announces-foldable-iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a> at a minimum. However, it appears that the feature is still in development, as they said the color temperature setting is not fully implemented.<br/>
<br/>
On the Mac, Edge Light illuminates the borders of the screen to evenly illuminate your face in low-light conditions. The feature is essentially a virtual ring light, and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/125934">there are settings</a> to adjust the width of the white line around the screen and a color temperature slider to make the light it produces appear more or less warm. <br/>
<br/>
